5] [17592186179725 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256714 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179725 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383012 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179725 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383019 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179725 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387269 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179725 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387276 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179725 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketching the Gradient Vector Field of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179725 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179587 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179725 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7a43f82e-6682-56af-994a-b0bdf12b4095" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179852 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180453 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179852 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180454 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179852 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180458 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179852 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180464 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179852 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180466 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179852 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179852 #edb/ref 1045 "Gradient Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179852 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180453 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179852 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050891 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186179852 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "563e73f4-5c65-5c01-b1d4-1909214d3b23" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180453 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180458 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180453 #edb/ref 1061 35370 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180453 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "42fa31ed-8f97-5aa3-89e8-9d271c417891" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180453 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180706 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180454 #edb/ref 1061 35372 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180454 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93326d12-a17a-5177-8940-426e5dd661b8" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180454 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179718 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180458 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180464 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180458 #edb/ref 1061 35387 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180458 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc32145d-a804-5c13-ba20-8531d24745d0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180458 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179720 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180464 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180466 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180464 #edb/ref 1061 35460 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180464 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f3f39866-fb9c-5fab-80a0-21104a970e7c" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180464 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179725 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180466 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180454 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180466 #edb/ref 1061 35468 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180466 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7dce8a1b-d2dc-5ba7-9445-52bd966f8de5" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180466 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180711 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180706 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that  if $f(x,y)$ is a function whose partial derivatives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y},\n$$\n\nexist and are continuous at a point $(x_0,y_0) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the gradient of $f$ evaluated at $(x_0, y_0)$ is given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) = \\dfrac{\\partial f }{\\partial x}(x_0, y_0) \\,\\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial f }{\\partial y} (x_0, y_0)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\, .\n$$\n\n \n\nNow suppose that the gradient vector exists at every point $(x,y)$ contained within the set $U\\subset \\mathbb R^2.$ Then, the **gradient vector field** of $f$ is defined as the map\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\longmapsto \\nabla f(x,y), \\qquad (x,y)\\in U.\n$$\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f(x,y)=x^2-y^2.$ To find the gradient vector field of $f,$ we proceed as follows.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =2x\\,i-2y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of this vector field is shown below.\n\n![](images/74/7437bd110810a0356a25afea897634c152b0441aee8e6403aa051dd2c1f205a7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180706 #edb/ref 1065 5764 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180706 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fe62959b-3e46-5af2-ba87-08f376c38b4e" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180706 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180711 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = x^2+y,$ shown below. How is this vector field related to the level curves of $f?$\n\n![](images/ef/ef5800723603b78beeb40c4352153f37298d4836905a6d8b751a919924664fa5.png)\n\nTo answer this question, we note the following:\n\n- As we've seen, the gradient of $f$ at a particular point $(x_0, y_0)$ lies *perpendicular* to the level curve $f(x,y) = f(x_0, y_0).$\n\n- Therefore, the gradient field vectors of $f$ at an arbitrary point $(x,y)$ lie perpendicular to its level curve at $(x,y).$\n\n- Moreover, the vectors always point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ is increasing.\n\nNow, if we superimpose the level curves of $f$ onto its vector field, we get a picture that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/5b/5bd2729b82312a50ee0c274fc1591545588f4881e2e279e80aca6b68f76ee824.png)\n\nNotice that the field vectors are indeed perpendicular to the level curves.\n\nWe can use a similar idea to sketch the vector field of a function using its level curves. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180711 #edb/ref 1065 5774 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180711 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60bb3f88-e435-5258-8b56-9c92481e7976" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186180711 #edb/ref 1067 "The Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186253677 #edb/ref 1081 "images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186253677 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186253677 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "00678095-4785-5a48-bfd2-04906406b23d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186253705 #edb/ref 1081 "images/4c/4c1a0035b37bc0137dc2ac164c980d538f9362feb3b439e663885f8aa7178124.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186253705 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186253705 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "03aaca50-6ba1-5767-8a1d-61289921be5f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186253945 #edb/ref 1081 "images/d3/d3fc823cfcea400219e553f9e6911a312f0a14c7f39aafb49d1af3463cb3e46b.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186253945 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186253945 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "1f1e18b0-1117-563b-8441-3490065e61e4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254275 #edb/ref 1081 "images/0e/0ea540eb4a48d1fb1777e2d6397e6d38937e9795c4d810b48c0fda0801d1c9ba.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254275 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254275 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "46e3f6a4-7353-56bc-8961-cc92fd971024" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254458 #edb/ref 1081 "images/fa/fa8bf282264f1647644cc15b6aece745718bd17617afad2c2530292bfed10b15.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254458 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254458 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "5936829d-c5a5-5317-93a8-8d046c07d07f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254578 #edb/ref 1081 "images/ba/ba42a974a78df2c0d228e8f5ae543019841c8a7b47e38f02e139fe2d97b8ecf5.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254578 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254578 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "64b24222-51a7-5272-9ca7-2db93a07e9b8" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254677 #edb/ref 1081 "images/70/704a1e0712c0c2512e16ac03f1eeb7ecd55996c822eced54ac510f4719485c42.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254677 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254677 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "70f6299c-8313-5e6f-a9c7-80abdfba5fd2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254901 #edb/ref 1081 "images/f4/f4f0b2e96fa7ee0f1dca7d57a2815e93d77665c0285fa81f2a8b2c5965a39415.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254901 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186254901 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "8ad5cb04-3d21-55c7-a532-d1bd2ca52b4a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255049 #edb/ref 1081 "images/f9/f9d6304bc9cc6b6e74235c66a7d70b96dbea2db44e9ed82606f6025075b76336.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255049 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255049 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "9bf9db65-02b1-5896-a31b-6345273456e5" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255371 #edb/ref 1081 "images/36/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255371 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255371 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "bde88cfd-9cb5-56e6-ba40-080493eb0ad6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255944 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186253945 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255944 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254275 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255944 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254578 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255944 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254901 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255944 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255371 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255944 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255944 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255944 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186255371 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186255944 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "0e297b46-c5d5-5311-943f-eb3962e376bf" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256227 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186253677 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256227 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186253705 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256227 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254458 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256227 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186254677 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256227 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186255049 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256227 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256227 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256227 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186253677 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256227 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "a46a2d28-3dee-5856-b9b2-7ac4fb2b706b" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256703 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186255944 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256703 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122693" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256703 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256703 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256703 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f30aab0-000a-5430-bdac-144d62e4296d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256703 #edb/ref 1074 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =x\\,i+y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,1) & =-\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(2,2) & =2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\,i+(-1)j=2\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/36/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256726 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256227 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256726 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122810" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256726 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256726 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256726 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f67d0b79-608f-51fc-afe3-9623997cda39" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186256726 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=-1,$ which gives the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=0,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=2,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/77/77a54ef31c41e0246e3d337a69c7faa161194058e6a5c672ef24d86866a83faf.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move downward and away from the origin $O.$ \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/4e/4e124d5e77ef9a28002aba9aac40f0b7eff1232dd1c08b6bca10b166469675c7.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n \n\n![](images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387276 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387277 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387276 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122732" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387276 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387276 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387276 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14b7ceda-a408-53c9-aeb5-05da06c84fba" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387276 #edb/ref 1074 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(2,1),$ and $(-2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1\\,j=i-4j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387277 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387278 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387277 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387279 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387277 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387280 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387277 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387281 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387277 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186387282 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387277 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387277 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387277 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186387281 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387277 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "c39f6b60-6db1-56ec-93e1-e634b438a208" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387278 #edb/ref 1081 "images/0c/0c425b6fcb88f4c958a7c0371a64a815eda5ebf8ed14b0adc06875de1e24003b.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387278 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387278 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "bec373fa-27cc-5734-871a-ec77749ea6a0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387279 #edb/ref 1081 "images/af/af7fdf48d36f477174867761b90d9349344029980012f1f7907f544efd3893bc.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387279 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387279 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "6120694d-f5d6-5a30-a9b1-04836470b4fd" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387280 #edb/ref 1081 "images/48/48c57f24904442c74178682db34aaa0113eb84042c78516a4e6f961e73b0f9eb.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387280 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387280 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "5738a2a6-58f2-5cb8-befa-f76d6d0fb2c7" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387281 #edb/ref 1081 "images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387281 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387281 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "11eee978-2cbc-57da-8a9a-9ffddf6422c7" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387282 #edb/ref 1081 "images/25/25ed4b4750662433bf4405b5e63ab78396688a985c76e2d34d449c0689830380.png" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387282 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387282 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "04551b05-601c-557a-a5a2-69cba4463197" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387310 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186387311 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387310 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122813" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387310 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186387310 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/50/50bd80ab9aa2f8d5ac2a453ccfe63815a75379efa7502cf1090eb015264206f6.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Whi